# Build context filter. Every image is built from the repository root -- runtime
# needs the sources and dev needs docker/vim/ -- so without this file the whole
# tree is uploaded to the daemon on every build, including a .venv and a pile of
# linter caches that no Dockerfile reads.
#
# This is *not* the same list as .gitignore, and copying one into the other
# would be a mistake in both directions. .gitignore answers "should this be
# committed"; this file answers "does any Dockerfile read this". Build outputs
# are excluded here because a stale local build/ directory silently landing in
# an image is worse than a slow upload.
#
# Note what this does not affect: an image's content hash. scripts/ci/images.py
# hashes the Dockerfile plus the explicit `inputs` globs from docker/images.yaml,
# so excluding a path here can never make a rebuild stop happening.

# CI metadata: read by nothing inside any image.
.github
.gitignore
.dockerignore

# `.git` is deliberately NOT excluded. runtime.Dockerfile does `COPY . .` and
# then configures CMake, which derives the version from `git describe --tags
# --dirty` at build time -- see the version invariant in CLAUDE.md. Excluding it
# would not fail the build; it would silently stamp every released binary and
# every output file's provenance with an unknown version, which is worse.

# Local development environments and tool caches
.venv
venv
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.pytest_cache
__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.cache
.vscode
.idea

# Build outputs -- CMake writes these at the repository root by default, and
# runtime.Dockerfile configures its own build directory inside the image.
build
build-*
out
docs/html
docs/latex
compile_commands.json

# Data and results: large, never an image input, and .h5 in particular is what
# the round-trip tests write next to the sources.
*.h5
*.hdf5
*.log
*.o
*.so
*.a

# Editor and OS noise
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
